From: hanson@hss.caltech.edu (Robin Hanson)
Date: Thu, 20 Oct 1994 10:30:50 -0700
Subject: [#94-10-368] SCI: Many-Worlds FAQ

Michael Clive Price writes:

>If we have:
>
>  |psi> = |1>*1 + |2>*3                (ignoring normalision)
>        = |1> + |2> + |2> + |2>
>
>then why not count world2 three times as often as world1?

What seems natural to me is to merge all identical worlds when
counting worlds.  To count the way you are indicating, you would have
to pick some "world size", and break bigger worlds up into worlds of
this size.  But then what do you do with smaller worlds?  If you could
deal with this somehow, you'd still find that in finite many-worlds,
almost all worlds have equiprobable history statistics, and in the the
infinite case all worlds would be below your world size cutoff -
meaning no worlds exist?

