From: davisd@nimitz.ee.washington.edu
Date: Thu, 20 Oct 94 15:51:27 -0700
Subject: [#94-10-376] PSYCH: Self Esteem

Nancy Lebovitz replies to my rejection of self esteem:
> 
> Part of what's gotten it onto my mind is the people who want
> to argue that self-esteem must be earned and that encouraging
> it in people who are doing badly is pernicious. The problem
> with that belief is that, if people don't value themselves, they
> have little reason to do better with their lives.

Of course, because they supposedly don't deserve it, because they are
bad people. We aren't nice to bad people, now are we?

If you don't mind living for a cause which is not your own, then
judging yourself as to how good you are *to that cause* is of course
quite natural. It is a serious conceptual mistake for an egoist to
judge *himself*. Presumably, to the extent an egoist has standards, he
recognizes those standards as *his own*; he recognizes himself as the
person who has chosen those standards. For what did he choose those
standards, save to further his own cause? I judge a type of food as
bad for me, so that I may know to avoid it in the future. Shall I
judge myself as bad? Do I plan on avoiding myself? 

In losing sight of who is doing the valuing, one loses the purpose of
values, eventually applying concepts of value where they do not
belong. An egoist might just as well call a thought purple as call
himself bad. I am not an object to be valued; I am the subject doing
the valuing.

